===LATIN SENTIMENT SCALE===
+3: EXTREMELY POSITIVE
- exsultatio (exultation), jubilum (jubilation), beatitudo (blessedness)
- summa felicitas (ultimate happiness), triumphus gloriosus (glorious triumph)

+2: VERY POSITIVE
- gaudium magnum (great joy), laetitia (gladness), amor (love)
- gloria (glory), victoria (victory), laus (praise), laudare (to praise)

+1: MODERATELY POSITIVE
- felix (fortunate/happy), laetus (happy), bonus (good)
- pulcher (beautiful), spes (hope), confidentia (confidence)

0: NEUTRAL
- Factual statements, descriptions without emotional content
- Administrative, legal, procedural language
- Objective observations

-1: MODERATELY NEGATIVE
- malus (bad), tristis (sad), anxius (anxious)
- timor (fear), sollicitudo (anxiety), dolor (pain/sorrow)

-2: VERY NEGATIVE
- dolor magnus (great pain), timor vehemens (intense fear)
- ira (anger), furor (rage/fury), odium (hatred)
- maeror (grief), calamitas (disaster)

-3: EXTREMELY NEGATIVE
- desperatio (despair), exitium (destruction), cruciatus (torture)
- tormentum (torment), summa miseria (utter wretchedness)
- malum extremum (extreme evil)

INTENSIFIERS (increase sentiment):
- valde (very, greatly), maxime (most, extremely), summe (supremely)
- vehementer (vehemently, intensely)
- Superlatives: -issimus/-errimus (adds +1 to base sentiment)

DIMINISHERS (decrease sentiment):
- aliquantum (somewhat), paulum (a little), leviter (lightly, slightly)

===GRAMMAR CODES===
NOM=Nominative (subject)
ACC=Accusative (direct object)
GEN=Genitive (possession)
DAT=Dative (indirect object)
ABL=Ablative (means, manner, place)
V=Verb
ADJ=Adjective
ADV=Adverb
CONJ=Conjunction
PREP=Preposition
PART=Participle
PRON=Pronoun

